## INTRODUCTION
Metal−organic cages $(\mathrm{MOCs})^{1-4}$ are a subset of discrete coordination complexes bearing well-defined cavities. They have proven useful in a range of applications, including molecular recognition,5−8 stereochemical sensin g,9,10 chemical separation,11−16 s tabilization of reactive species,17 and catalysis.18−24 In particular, the construction of spin-crossover (SCO) coordination cage s,25−35 of which the metal centers are capable of switching between high-spin and low-spin states upon external stimuli, have received a growing interest. The magnetic switching of cages accompanied by the length variations of coordination bonds provides a new means to modulate the cavity volumes and therefore the guest binding properties.36 It also enriches the function of cages and extends their potential applications for molecular switches and optical sensors.37−42 Nevertheless, the preparation of SCO cages to date is limited to the use of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ with azole-based ligands $^{29-33,36}$ which afford appropriate ligand fields for SCO. The exploration of new approaches to construct SCO cages and to control SCO behaviors remains challenging. It is even more challenging to find a way to turn a SCO-inactive cage into an active one.
On the other hand, encapsulation of discrete nanocages within mesoporous materials has become an emerging research are a.43−51 The cages confined in solid porous matrices can show enhanced stability, dispersity, and cavity accessibility, offering the chance to modulate their physical/chemical properties.52,53 Foundational work has provided interesting examples of encapsulation of MOCs within mesoporous silicas,43 carbon materials,44 or metal−organic frameworks (MOFs),45,46 demonstrating improved performances in adsorption, catalysis, or proton conduction in comparison with pure MOCs. Nevertheless, the confined self-assembly strategy is still challenged by the selection of compatible cages and porous matrices and the lack of efficient approaches for high cage loadings.
Similarly, integration of magnetic molecules into porous matrices has been studied to probe the magnetic effects of confinement and molecular segregation and to obtain nanostructured functional materials.5 Single-molecule magnets (SMMs)56−63 and SCO complexes64−73 have been incorporated into zeolites,6 4 mesoporous silicas,56−58,65−68 carbon nanotubes,59 and MOFs.60−63,69−73 To the best of our knowledge, however, the magnetic effects of confining MOCs within porous solids have not yet been reported to date.
The confinement of a MOC within a porous solid can produce a hierarchical host−guest structure unavailable in the above studies: the confined MOC serves as a guest of the porous solid and meanwhile can host its own guest, and the apertures between the MOC and the inner surface of the porous solid can provide a special space for hosting other guests. We envisioned that the confinement of cages and multilevel host−guest interactions could provide multiple pathways to activate and control the spin state of cages. In this work, we demonstrate such concept through incarceration of anionic $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cages within the mesopores of a cationic MOF (MIL-101). Remarkably, in contrast with the pure cage that exhibits invariant low-spin $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ , the confinement within porous matrices enables the cage to display stimuli-responsive spin-state switching.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Design and Synthesis of MOC@MOF
In-situ assembly inside confined spaces, or confined selfassembly, is an efficient strategy to incorporate MOCs into porous solids.43−46 The mesopores of the solid have windows of limited size, which allow the entrance of the assembly components of a target cage but prevent the in-situ assembled cage from leaking out. To have strong driving forces for high loading, here, we adopt a charge-assisted approach, which involves MOFs and MOCs with opposite charges to reinforce the encapsulation.
A cationic $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ −terephthalate MOF, MIL-101 with ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ counterions,74 was chosen as the host matrix, with an anionic tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}\mathrm{MOC}^{17,75}$ as the confined guest, where $\mathrm{~L~}$ is the Schiff-base ligand formed from 2-formylpyridine and a disulfonate-anionized diamine (Figure 1). MIL-101 shows a water-resistant framework and holds two types of hydrophilic mesoporous cage-like compartments with internal diameters of 2.9 and $3.4~\mathrm{nm}$ , accessible through microporous windows of 1.2 and $1.6~\ \mathrm{nm}$ .76 The water-soluble $\ensuremath{\hat{\mathrm{Fe}}}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{L}}_{6}$ cage has a peripheral diameter of $2.5~\mathrm{\nm}$ . The size of the nanocage relative to the windows and compartments of the MOF indicates that each compartment can trap one cage, leaving obvious spaces between the framework surface and cage. The target material, named $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ , is an ionic compound with the nanocages serving as large counteranions for the cationic framework.
The MOF was hydrothermally synthesized from $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}$ and terephthalic acid.74 The formation of the cationic MIL-101 framework and the presence/exchangeability of the nitrate counterions were confirmed by X-ray diffraction (XRD), anion chromatography, and anion exchange experiments (Section 3 of the Supporting Information). To prepare $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101,$ the subcomponent self-assembly of the cage in the presence of MIL-101 was performed by stirring solid MIL-101 in an aqueous solution containing $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -diaminobiphenyl- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -disulfonic acid (neutralized with $\mathrm{NMe}_{4}\mathrm{OH}$ ), 2-formylpyridine, and $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}$ . Driven by anion exchange with ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ and coordination with $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ , the in-situ-formed anionic pyridylimine ligand can enter the compartments of MIL-101 and assemble into the anionic nanocages. The resultant violet solid isolated by filtration was washed with deionized water to remove any contaminants (including the soluble cage compound that may be adsorbed on solid surfaces). The reaction in the absence of MIL-101 led to the cage in solution, which was isolated as $[\mathrm{NMe}_{4}]_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}]$ upon addition of acetone.17,75 The characterization of the cage is presented in Section 4 of the Supporting Information. Particularly, ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements reveal a type II isotherm (Figure S5B), typical of nonporous solids that show multilayer surface adsorption. The nonporous nature is because the cages and the countercations are packed without void space in between and the windows of the cage cavity are blocked in the aggregated solid.
## Structural Characterization of MOC@MOF
The Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@$ MIL-101 shows the characteristic peaks of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage in the region of $1285{-}965~\mathrm{~cm^{-1}}$ (Figure 2A), suggesting the successful integration of the cages into the MOF. Concomitantly, the characteristic peak of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ $\cdot1385~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ observed for MIL-101 is obviously weakened in $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ , indicating that the anionic nanocages replace some nitrate anions to serve as counterions for the cationic framework of MIL-101. The solid-state $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ CP (cross-polarization) MAS (magic angle spinning) NMR spectrum shows a series of broadened signals in the region of $110{-}185\mathrm{ppm},$ including the resolved imine peak at $175\mathrm{ppm}$ , which are characteristic of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage (Figure 2B). These peaks are broad, presumably due to the existence of paramagnetic $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ in MIL-101 and high-spin $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ in the encapsulated MOC (see the discussion below). No peak for $\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{N}^{+}$ was observed in the spectrum, confirming that the cages are not in the form of $[\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{N}]_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}]$ but serve as counterions for the cationic framework. As a charge-balancing ingredient of the compound, the counterions should be encapsulated within the mesoporous compartments rather than adsorbed on solid surfaces. The positive electrostatic field generated by the cationic framework affords an attractive force for the compartments to accommodate the anionic cages.
Compared with MIL-101, $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ shows no obvious change in the XRD peak positions, demonstrating the preservation of the crystalline MIL-101 framework after cage loading (Figure 2C). The significant differences in the relative intensity of the diffraction peaks indicate the changes of atomic distribution in the lattice of MIL-101 and support the encapsulation of the MOCs within the pores.76,77 According to scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figures 2D and S8), the octahedral morphology and the submicrometer size of the crystals of MIL-101 are well reserved after cage encapsulation, with no indication of surface contamination. Energy dispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spectroscopic (EDS) elemental mapping presents homogeneous distribution of Fe, N, and S of $\bar{\mathrm{Fe}}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\bar{\mathrm{L}_{6}}$ over the crystals.
Thermogravimetric measurements present a higher decomposition temperature of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\dot{\mathrm{MIL}}\ –101$ $36\bar{1}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ than those of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}4\dot{\mathrm{L}}_{6}$ and MIL-101 (315 and $327^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively) (Figure S7), which proves the improved stability of the MOC after encapsulation.
The chemical composition of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}.101$ was determined using several methods, including NMR, ICPAES, ion chromatography, and elemental analysis (Table S1). The molar ratio of the disulfonate ligand to Fe was found to be
1.6, in good agreement with the expected ratio (1.5). This value provides quantitative proof for the formation of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage. The analytical data support the composition of $\big[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}\mathrm{L}_{3}^{\prime}\big]\big[\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}\big]_{0.075}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{0.7}$ The nanocages serve as large counteranions to neutralize $30\%$ positive charge of the framework. The analytic results in combination with structural data of MIL- ${101}^{76}$ reveal that $85\%$ compartments in MIL-101 (about 20 compartments out of the 24 compartments per unit cell) are loaded with the cages. The detailed discussion is presented in Section 5.1 of the Supporting Information.
The material remains highly porous after cage encapsulation, with somewhat decreased ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption capacity and Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area $\left(\mathrm{from}\ 2830\right.$ to $2180\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ (Figure 2E). The decrease in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption seems to be less significant than expected for the occupancy of up to $85\%$ of compartments in MIL-101 by cages. However, considering the relative size of the compartments and cages (Figure 1), there are still large apertures between the framework and cage after cage loading, which is responsible for the high $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption of the cage-loaded material. A similar trend is observed in water vapor sorption (Figure 2F), with the capacity showing a moderate decrease from 56 to 43 water molecules per $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ unit. In contrast to the single-step rapid absorption/desorption by MIL-101, the water sorption isotherm of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ displays a two-step adsorption/ desorption. The slow adsorption at low humidity is presumably associated with the encapsulated cages, which possess hydrophilic surfaces owing to the sulfonate groups. The second adsorption step is abrupt and reveals a rapid pore-filling process, with the humidity range and the desorption hysteresis similar to those observed for MIL-101. The similarity reflects the relevance of the framework surface to the second adsorption process.
## Confinement-Evoked SCO Behaviors
We observed reversible color change between gray and violet upon water desorption/adsorption of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}^{-}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ (Figure 2F), which we infer results from spin-state switching of the encapsulated $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage. To demonstrate the spin crossover behavior, we measured the temperature-dependent m a g n e t i c s u s c e p t i b i l i t i e s f o r $\mathrm{Fe}^{\hat{\mathrm{II}}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}-101}.$ $[\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{N}]_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}],$ , and MIL-101. As shown in Figure 3A, the cage compound is diamagnetic (low-spin (LS), $\mathrm{Fe^{II}{}^{1}{A}_{1})}$ over the whole temperature range. In contrast, the $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ product $(\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ is the magnetic susceptibility per mole of $\mathrm{Cr}$ ) of the assynthesized $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ shows a smooth increase upon increasing temperature from $2\mathrm{~K},$ followed by a sigmoidal increase above $300~\mathrm{K}.$ The low-temperature $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T{-}T$ profile is almost coincident with that of MIL-101, where the antiferromagnetic exchange within the $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ unit is responsible for the smooth increase of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ with temperature.78 The magnetic discrepancy between $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}\ –1\bar{0}1$ and MIL-101 indicates the presence of high-spin (HS) $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\ \left(^{5}\mathrm{T}_{2}\right)$ at high temperatures, and the sigmoidal increase suggests the occurrence of an LS-to-HS spin transition for the confined cage.
For a close inspection of the spin-crossover behavior, the susceptibility of the confined $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}4^{\sum_{6}}$ cage was derived from the susceptibilities of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}\dot{1}0\dot{1}$ and MIL-101. The cage and the MOF can be assumed to be magnetically independent because the magnetic interactions between the far-separated $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ centers should be negligible (if any). Accordingly, the susceptibility difference $\Delta\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ between $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}\bar{1}01$ and MIL-101 corresponds to the susceptibility of the confined $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage. For convenience, $\Delta\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ was divided by the $\mathrm{Fe/Cr}$ molar ratio (0.10; see Table S1) to convert the values per mole of Cr to the values per mole of Fe. The $\Delta\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T{-}T$ curves thus obtained are shown in Figure 3B.
As can be seen, $\Delta\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ shows a slow increase with the temperature increasing from 80 to $300~\mathrm{~K},$ indicative of a gradual LS-to-HS conversion. A rapid transition occurs above $300~\mathrm{~K},$ suggesting spin crossover with some degree of cooperativity. The $\Delta\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ value of $3.1~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{~K~mol}^{-1}$ at $400~\mathrm{K}$ is consistent with $\mathrm{HS~Fe^{II}}$ .79 The magnetic change with the temperature is irreversible. The cooling process witnesses a slow decrease of $\Delta\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ in the temperature range from 400 to $100~\mathrm{K},$ followed by a constant region from 100 to $30~\mathrm{K}$ The decrease indicates a gradual and incomplete HS-to-LS conversion of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}^{\mathrm{{c}}}}$ centers rather than the magnetic interactions between HS $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ centers. The distances between $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers in the cage are up to $13\mathrm{~\AA},$ and any magnetic interactions between these centers would be too weak to show appreciable magnetic effects in the temperature region concerned. The rapid decrease of $\Delta\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ upon cooling below $30~\mathrm{K}$ can be attributed to the zero-field splitting of remaining HS FeII,31 with possible contributions from magnetic interactions between metal centers.
The spin transition during heating above $300~\mathrm{K}$ is associated with the release of water from the material, in line with the rapid initial weight loss in the thermogravimetric plot (Figure S7). The water-induced spin crossover is consistent with the color change of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\hat{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}101$ upon water desorption and adsorption (Figure 2F). The effect of water on spin states is also confirmed by isothermal magnetization at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S9): the dehydrated sample shows higher magnetization than the pristine one.
The spin change upon dehydration is also evidenced by several spectroscopic characterization. (i) Solid-state $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ MAS NMR spectra of dehydrated $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101$ presents unusual deshielded and broadened signals with significant spinning sidebands in comparison with that of the hydrated sample (Figure S10), in agreement with the LS-to-HS conversion upon dehydration. (ii) The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectrum of the dehydrated $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{\widehat{Fe}}_{\mathbf{\Phi}_{4}}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathbf{L}_{6}@\mathbf{M}\mathbf{L}.}\end{array}$ 101 shows increased and decreased binding energies, respectively, for Fe 2p and imine N 1s (Figure S11), compared with that of the LS cage, in line with the composition of HS $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ . (iii) Solid-state ultraviolet−visible (UV−vis) absorption spectra show a decrease of the maximum at $580~\mathrm{nm}$ and the (diFsiagpuprearSa1n2c)e, of etshpe bduinmgp oa $530~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ ahfitnegr dfethyed $\mathrm{Fe_{\LS}^{I I}}$ $^{1}\mathrm{{A}_{1}^{-}\rightarrow^{1}\mathrm{{E}}}$ and $^{1}\mathbf{A}_{1}\hat{\rightarrow}^{1}\mathbf{A}_{2}$ transitions.80,81 The appearance of the bump at $365~\mathrm{nm}$ and the increased absorption above 630 nm indicate the metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT) and ${}^{5}\mathrm{T}_{2}\rightarrow{}^{5}\mathrm{E}$ transitions of $\mathrm{Fe_{\HS}^{\mathrm{\bfM}}}$ .82,83
To quantitatively probe the spin states, $^{57}\mathrm{Fe}$ Mössbauer spectroscopy was recorded at 298 K with the specially prepared $^{57}\mathrm{Fe}$ material (see Section 2.4 in the Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 4A, the gray sample of dehydrated $^{57}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{}\cdot101$ presents two quadrupole-split doublets corresponding to $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{HS}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ $\left(\Delta E_{\mathrm{Q}}=2.5~\mathrm{mm}/s\right.$ , $\delta=1.14$ $\mathbf{mm}/s\mathbf{\dot{\eta}},$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{\LS}}^{\mathrm{I\hat{I}}}$ $\left(\Delta E_{\mathrm{Q}}=0.57\ \mathrm{mm}/s\right.$ , $\mathbf{\bar{\delta}}$ ) with a ratio of 37:63. As expected, the violet hydrated sample contains a higher fraction of $\mathrm{\bar{F}e}_{\mathrm{\LS}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ( $86\%$ , Figure 4B). The presence of a minor portion of $\mathrm{Fe_{\HS}^{I I}}$ in the hydrated sample is consistent with the slow spin conversion revealed by increasing susceptibility from 80 to $300~\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 3B).
## Effects of Guest inside Confined MOCs
Interestingly, the spin state can be further modulated through the host−guest chemistry of the confined cage. Benzene has been demonstrated to be a suitable guest for the $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage through hydrophobic interactions.17,75,84 When benzene was added to the aqueous solution during the assembly of $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}4\mathrm{L}_{6}@$ MIL-101, a material with a benzene guest $(\mathrm{BZ}@\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}.$ 101) was obtained. The guest is detectable after extraction with chloroform (Figure S13). In contrast, no benzene was detected for the control experiment with MIL-101. The comparison suggests the essential importance of the cage for effective benzene inclusion and preservation of the guest binding properties of the cage after encapsulation. Because the cage possesses a hydrophilic outer surface and a hydrophobic cavity, it is reasonable that the benzene molecules are captured inside the cage, as reported in the literature.17,75,84
The Mössbauer spectrum of hydrated $\mathrm{BZ}@^{57}\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}.$ 101 suggests that all $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions are in the LS state (Figure 5A), in line with the deeper violet color compared with the benzene-free compound (inset of Figure 4B). Release of benzene and water upon heating $\mathrm{BZ}@\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{~}4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}.101$ leads to the color change to gray, as observed for dehydration of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ . Consistently, magnetic measurements confirm the fully LS state of $\mathrm{BZ}@\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}{}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MLL}{}-101$ below $300~\mathrm{K}$ and reveal a spin transition to HS above $300\mathrm{K}$ upon release of the benzene and water guests (Figure 5C). The evacuated material shows a magnetic behavior very similar to that of dehydrated $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{\check{M}I L}.101$ upon a temperature decrease (Figure 3A). Notably, compared with confined $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ (Figure 3B), the spin transition of confined ${\mathrm{BZ}}(\varpi{\mathrm{Fe}}^{{\mathrm{II}}}{}_{4}{\mathrm{L}}_{6}$ (Figure 5D) is sharper and the critical temperature is higher (375 versus 330 K). These results demonstrate that the SCO behavior of the material can be controlled by the innermost guest in the hierarchical structure (the guest enclosed within the MOFconfined cage).
## Proposed Mechanisms and Sensing Application
The guest-relevant SCO behaviors of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage confined within MIL-101 can be interpreted by multilevel host−guest interactions as detailed below. (i) For the pure cage, the nonporous solid lattice composed of aggregated cages and cations stabilizes $\mathrm{LS\Fe^{II}}$ and restricts the bond elongation required for the LS-to-HS conversion. (ii) In $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}.$ 101, the cages are singly quarantined inside the compartments of MIL-101. The water guests filling the spaces between cages and compartment walls (Figure 4C) are capable of stabilizing LS $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ through solvation, analogous to what happens to the solvated cage in aqueous solution.17,75,84 However, once guest water is removed, the cage is offered the freedom of expansion through bond elongation around $\mathrm{Fe^{II}}$ , which leads to $\mathrm{\hat{H}S~F e^{\mathrm{II}}}$ . The driving force for cage expansion could be the electrostatic interactions between the anionic cages and the cationic walls of the MOF, and the hydrogen-bonding interactions between the sulfonate groups in the cage and the coordinatively bound water molecules at the internal surfaces of the MOF could also contribute. The phenomenon can also be understood by considering the matrix pressure suffered by the cage. The rigid MOF matrix filled with water guests imposes an effective local pressure on the encapsulated cage, which stabilizes the LS state. Without water guests, however, the cage in the relatively large compartment does not experience strong local pressure but experiences charge-assisted attractive interactions from the compartment walls, so the HS state is favored. The “solventassisted matrix effect“ has been proposed to explain the solvent-triggered spin state switching of a matrix-confined mononuclear $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complex.71 (iii) When benzene is included in the cavity of the cage (Figure 5B), the hydrophobic host−guest interactions have a tendency to prevent the cage from expanding and thereby further stabilize the LS state. Therefore, $\mathrm{B}\bar{\mathrm{Z}}@\mathrm{Fe}_{\mathrm{~}4}^{\mathrm{II}^{\sim}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}.1\dot{0}1$ is LS below $300~\mathrm{~K~},$ and upon desolvation, it exhibits a steeper spin transition and a higher critical temperature than the counterpart material without benzene inside the cage.
The reversible water-induced color change of ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}{\mathrm{L}}_{6}@{\mathrm{MIL}}-$ 101 observed above reveals the potential of the SCO material for colorimetric humidity sensing. As shown in Figure $\mathrm{6A},$ after exposure of dehydrated $\mathbf{\dot{F}e^{I I}}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}.101$ to the air at relative humidity (RH) from $33\%$ to $75\%$ , linear relationships between absorbance changes and RH were observed, implying the capability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\bar{\mathrm{L}}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ for accurate RH determination. The sample could be recovered and reused at least five times with no indication of fatigue (Figure S14). In particular, no noticeable color response was observed when dehydrated $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ was exposed to the vapors of common volatile organic solvents (Figure 6B), demonstrating the high selectivity of the material for the colorimetric sensing of atmospheric water.
## CONCLUSION
In summary, we assembled a hierarchical host−guest structure through confined self-assembly of an anionic $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage within the cationic framework of MIL-101. The confinement effects of the MOF enable the $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}$ cage to exhibit stimuliresponsive magnetic behaviors. Contrary to the pure cage compound with an invariant diamagnetic LS ground state, the cage confined inside MIL-101 shows temperature-dependent and guest-modulable SCO phenomena. These results illustrate the intriguing magnetic effects of outer porous host matrices and inner guests on metal−organic cages in hierarchical host− guest systems. The distinct color change upon spin conversion allows for the selective colorimetric sensing of atmospheric water. The work offers new perspectives for developing supramolecular materials with sophisticated host−guest structures and functions.
## METHODS
## Synthesis of $\mathsf{F e}_{4}^{11}\mathsf{L}_{6}@\mathsf{M l L}\cdot101$
$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Diaminobiphenyl- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -disulfonic acid $85\%$ , hydrate, $33.7\mathrm{mg},$ $0.0825~\mathrm{\mmol}_{.}$ ) and tetramethylammonium hydroxide pentahydrate $\left(30.0\mathrm{\mg},0.165\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ were dissolved under stirring in deionized water $(2{\mathrm{~mL}})$ in a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ Schlenk tube. 2-Formylpyridine $(16~\mu\mathrm{L},$ $0.165~\mathrm{\mmol};$ and $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(15.3\mathrm{~\mg},0.055\mathrm{~\mmol}\right)$ were successively added under stirring. To the resultant dark violet solution was added MIL-101 powder $\mathrm{'}190\ \mathrm{mg},0.312\ \mathrm{mmol}$ , based on $\mathrm{Cr})$ ). The tube was sealed and filled with nitrogen by three evacuation/ charge cycles. The heterogeneous reaction mixture was stirred at 50 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then filtered to give a violet powder, which was washed with deionized water $(2\ \mathrm{~mL~}\times\ 5)$ to remove possible adsorbates on the solid surfaces. The solid was dried in air (yield, 160 mg, $86\%$ based on MIL-101). The analytic results are provided and discussed in Section 5.1 of the Supporting Information. $^{57}\mathrm{Fe}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathrm{L}_{6}@$ MIL-101 for Mössbauer spectroscopy was synthesized from elemental 57Fe as described in the Supporting Information.
